---
// Plain-language key to the score columns (spec 12.8, docs/score-normalization.md).
interface Props { open?: boolean }
const { open = false } = Astro.props;
---
<details class="explainer" open={open}>
  <summary>How to read the scores</summary>
  <p>A CRISPR screen knocks out one gene per cell, lets the cells grow (or treats them), and then counts which
    knockouts became rarer or more common. Every study reports this in its own unit, so we add three shared
    yardsticks next to the original number. Throughout the site, <strong>low means depleted</strong>: cells
    without the gene grew worse. We flip a study's sign where needed so this always holds.</p>
  <dl>
    <dt>Rank (level A), 0 to 1</dt>
    <dd>The gene's position among all genes in that one screen: 0 = the most depleted gene, 0.5 = the middle,
      1 = the most enriched. Like a finishing position in a race given as a percentile. It works for almost every
      screen, but it says nothing about <em>how much</em> the cells were affected.</dd>
    <dt>Scale (level B), anchored on known genes</dt>
    <dd>A two-point calibration, like a standard curve with a blank and a positive control.
      <b>0</b> = the median of a reference set of hundreds of genes known to be non-essential (the blank).
      <b>−1</b> = the median of a reference set of core-essential genes, such as ribosome and DNA-replication
      genes (the positive control). So −0.5 means "half as depleted as a typical essential gene", below −1 means
      "more depleted than a typical essential gene", and above 0 means the cells grew <em>better</em> without it.
      Only screens that select for growth and contain enough reference genes get this scale.</dd>
    <dt>Robust z (level C)</dt>
    <dd>How many typical spreads the gene sits from the middle of its own screen, like flagging an outlier well
      against the plate median. −3 is unusually depleted <em>for that screen</em>. Useful for drug and sorting
      screens, but not comparable in size between screens.</dd>
    <dt>Native score</dt>
    <dd>The study's own number in its own unit (log2 fold change, MAGeCK, BAGEL Bayes factor, CERES and others),
      like readings from different instruments. Never compare native scores between studies.</dd>
    <dt>Hit</dt>
    <dd>Whether the study's authors called the gene significant, by their own threshold.</dd>
    <dt>Comparable as</dt>
    <dd><b>anchored</b>: has rank, scale and z. <b>robust z</b>: rank and z only. <b>rank only</b>: rank only.
      <b>not comparable</b>: we could not tell which direction is "depleted" (or the study only listed its hits),
      so only the native score is shown.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>What the numbers do <em>not</em> tell you yet: <em>why</em> a gene matters in one cell line and not another.
    That depends on the line's own mutations, expression and copy number, which we plan to add next.
    Method details: <a href="https://github.com/ricskoch/data-webpage/blob/main/docs/score-normalization.md">score
    normalization notes</a>.</p>
</details>
